{"record_id": "entry-5984", "entry_number": 5984, "assignment_position": 1107, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Trump Lawyer Solicited $1 Million from Qatar, Which Declined", "description_medium": "A 2018 Washington Post report said Trump lawyer Michael Cohen solicited at least $1 million from Qatar in late 2016, offering access and advice about the incoming administration. The government declined. The report does not say Qatar donated money to Trump’s inaugural committee, so the original title’s claim of an accepted foreign-government gift is unsupported.", "description_long": "Before Trump took office in 2017, Michael Cohen, his personal attorney, approached a Qatari representative about a payment of at least $1 million, according to a 2018 Washington Post investigation. The reported offer was tied to access to and advice about the incoming administration; Qatar declined it. The article placed the approach around a December 2016 meeting at Trump Tower between Qatar’s foreign minister and members of the transition team, including national security adviser-designate Michael Flynn and incoming chief strategist Steve Bannon. Cohen spoke separately with Ahmed al-Rumaihi, then an official of Qatar’s sovereign wealth fund. This is materially different from saying that the Qatari government made, or Trump’s inaugural committee accepted, a donation. Separate reporting on inauguration fundraising described large donations by American individuals and companies, including donors with overseas business relationships, but those facts do not convert Cohen’s unsuccessful solicitation into a foreign-government contribution. The source documents an alleged offer and its refusal; it does not establish a completed payment or a constitutional violation.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "Trump Inaugural Fund Accepts $1 Million from Foreign Government — Emoluments Violation", "reason": "Corrects the central factual error: the source reports a declined solicitation by Cohen, not a $1 million donation accepted by Trump’s inaugural fund."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/national-security/trumps-personal-attorney-solicited-1-million-from-government-of-qatar/2018/05/16/e787e716-592c-11e8-858f-12becb4d6067_story.html"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-11-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["The referenced New York Times article concerns a donor under scrutiny, but it does not substantiate a Qatari government gift. Washington Post reported the Qatar solicitation and that the offer was declined."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Corrects the central factual error: the source reports a declined solicitation by Cohen, not a $1 million donation accepted by Trump’s inaugural fund.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 55, "description_long": 166}}
{"record_id": "entry-5989", "entry_number": 5989, "assignment_position": 1109, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Trump Shares AI-Generated Images Falsely Depicting Taylor Swift Support", "description_medium": "In August 2024, Trump shared AI-generated images on Truth Social that falsely showed Taylor Swift and apparent fans supporting his presidential campaign. He captioned one post “I accept!” Swift had not endorsed him. The reporting documents Trump sharing the images; it does not establish that his campaign created them or that Swift’s likeness was used with her consent.", "description_long": "On August 18, 2024, Trump reposted several AI-generated images on Truth Social that depicted Taylor Swift and women dressed as “Swifties” endorsing his campaign. One image used a false caption claiming Swift fans were turning to Trump after a thwarted concert attack; Trump added “I accept!” to the post. Other images showed fabricated campaign-style scenes. Swift had not endorsed Trump, and the images were not genuine photographs of her endorsing him. The Guardian reported that some images appeared to be made with generative AI and that the episode intensified concern about synthetic media being used in election messaging. The article also described a separate false image involving a fake endorsement, which should not be confused with an actual statement from Swift or her representatives. The available reporting supports saying Trump shared deceptive AI imagery. It does not show that campaign staff created the images, that every image was AI-generated, or that a formal campaign ad used them. Swift publicly endorsed Kamala Harris weeks later, after the Democratic convention.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "Trump Campaign Uses AI Deepfakes of Taylor Swift Endorsing Him", "reason": "Replaces the unsupported claim that the campaign generated deepfakes with the documented act of Trump sharing misleading AI imagery."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/19/trump-ai-swift-harris-musk-deepfake-images", "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/24/trump-taylor-swift-deepfakes-ai"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/chatgpt-web/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-01-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["Used Guardian’s event report and follow-up analysis; wording distinguishes Trump sharing the images from their creation or formal campaign use."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Replaces the unsupported claim that the campaign generated deepfakes with the documented act of Trump sharing misleading AI imagery.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 58, "description_long": 168}}
{"record_id": "entry-5996", "entry_number": 5996, "assignment_position": 1111, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Pro-Trump Poll Watchers Prepared to Challenge Ballots in Wisconsin", "description_medium": "A Reuters report published before the 2024 election described pro-Trump poll watchers preparing to monitor ballot counting in Wisconsin. Election officials warned that organized challenges, like those seen during the 2020 count, could slow mail-ballot processing. The article reports preparations and officials’ concerns; it does not establish that a court found a coordinated voter-intimidation scheme.", "description_long": "In late October 2024, Reuters reported that pro-Trump groups were recruiting and preparing poll watchers for Election Day in Wisconsin. The article focused on Milwaukee-area ballot counting, where activists who believed Trump’s false claims about the 2020 election had previously challenged mail ballots. Wisconsin election officials interviewed by Reuters warned that a much larger wave of challenges could disrupt or delay the processing of results in a pivotal state. A nonpartisan Brennan Center lawyer told Reuters that some observers were primed to interpret routine counting activity as evidence of wrongdoing. Poll watchers may observe election procedures under state rules, while challenges and objections are subject to legal limits and local administration. The warning concerned possible disruption of ballot counting and delays in reporting results in a closely watched state, not a finding that voters had already been turned away. Poll watchers can observe procedures, while election officials retain responsibility for determining ballot eligibility under state rules. The report framed the issue as preparation and risk before Election Day.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "Trump Deploys Supporters as 'Poll Watchers' — Voter Intimidation at the Polls", "reason": "Separates documented preparations and prospective risk from unsupported assertions that widespread intimidation or unlawful challenges had already occurred."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.reuters.com/world/us/pro-trump-poll-watchers-primed-election-day-action-key-state-2024-10-31/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/lightsprint/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-04-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["Reuters’ Wisconsin report supports watcher preparations and election officials’ warnings, but not the supplied claims of widespread 2024 intimidation or court orders."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Separates documented preparations and prospective risk from unsupported assertions that widespread intimidation or unlawful challenges had already occurred.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 55, "description_long": 168}}
{"record_id": "entry-6013", "entry_number": 6013, "assignment_position": 1113, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "New York Attorney General Alleges Trump Overstated Net Worth by Up to $2.2 Billion", "description_medium": "In an August 2023 court filing, New York Attorney General Letitia James alleged that Trump overstated his net worth by between $812 million and $2.2 billion in particular years from 2011 to 2021. The figures appeared in a motion seeking partial summary judgment in the state’s civil fraud case. At that point they were allegations, not findings by the trial judge.", "description_long": "New York Attorney General Letitia James filed a civil fraud lawsuit in 2022 against Trump, several Trump Organization entities and senior executives, alleging that annual financial statements misstated asset values. In an August 2023 motion for partial summary judgment, her office said Trump’s reported net worth was overstated by amounts ranging from $812 million to $2.2 billion, depending on the year, between 2011 and 2021. The filing cited properties including Trump Tower and Mar-a-Lago and argued that inflated valuations helped secure favorable loan and insurance terms. Trump denied wrongdoing and challenged the state’s calculations and legal theory. A motion asks a court to decide a claim before trial; the attorney general’s figures were not, at that point, a judicial finding. The case later proceeded to trial, where the court addressed liability and remedies under New York law. The episode should therefore be identified as the attorney general’s allegation in a court filing, with the size and period attributed to her office, rather than as a finding made by the attorney general or a final determination on that date.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "NY AG Finds Trump Financial Statements Fraudulently Inflated by $2.2 Billion", "reason": "Corrects the attribution and procedural status of the $2.2 billion figure, which was presented by the attorney general in a motion before the judge ruled."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.nytimes.com/2023/08/30/nyregion/trump-james-civil-lawsuit-trial.html", "https://apnews.com/article/88379aaf16849c255365ff871384ff7e"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/native-cloud/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-06-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["The original New York Times event report and Associated Press coverage describe James’s 2023 motion as allegations; the supplied synopsis prematurely calls them findings."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Corrects the attribution and procedural status of the $2.2 billion figure, which was presented by the attorney general in a motion before the judge ruled.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 61, "description_long": 178}}
{"record_id": "entry-6024", "entry_number": 6024, "assignment_position": 1115, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Trump-Backed Mehmet Oz and Doug Mastriano Lose Pennsylvania Races", "description_medium": "On November 8, 2022, Democrat John Fetterman defeated Republican Mehmet Oz in Pennsylvania’s U.S. Senate race, and Democrat Josh Shapiro defeated Doug Mastriano for governor. Trump had endorsed both Republican candidates and campaigned for them. The results establish that both lost; they do not by themselves show that Trump’s endorsements caused either defeat.", "description_long": "Pennsylvania voters rejected two candidates endorsed by Trump in the November 2022 midterm election. John Fetterman defeated Mehmet Oz for the state’s open U.S. Senate seat, while Josh Shapiro beat Doug Mastriano in the governor’s race. Trump had endorsed Oz during the Republican primary and later campaigned for him; he also supported Mastriano, who had promoted false claims about the 2020 election. Fetterman’s campaign took place while he was recovering from a stroke, and Oz’s candidacy drew national attention as Republicans sought to regain control of the Senate. Mastriano lost by a wider margin than Oz. The paired results became part of Republican debate about candidate selection and Trump’s influence, but an election outcome alone cannot establish that the endorsement determined the result. The packet’s linked AP URL is unrelated to these races, so this account uses event-specific coverage and the election results. It describes the defeats without claiming that either race was considered safely winnable or that Trump personally caused the losses.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "Trump-Endorsed Pennsylvania Candidates Oz and Mastriano Both Lose", "reason": "Replaces the unrelated source and removes the unsupported causal claim that Trump’s endorsements cost Republicans otherwise-winnable seats."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/philadelphia/news/pa-senate-election-results-fetterman-defeats-oz-cbs-news/", "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/trump-backed-mastriano-concedes-pennsylvania-governor-race-2022-11-14/"], "r…330 tokens truncated…o government records and classified material. On August 11, Attorney General Merrick Garland announced that the Justice Department had asked a federal judge to unseal the warrant and property receipt, noting Trump had publicly confirmed the search and that it was in the public interest. Trump then said he would not oppose release of the warrant-related records and called for their immediate disclosure. A separate request from news organizations sought the underlying affidavit, which describes evidence offered to establish probable cause. The Justice Department opposed unsealing that affidavit, arguing that disclosure could expose witnesses and harm the ongoing investigation. A judge later ordered a redacted version released. The distinction matters because the source title’s suggestion that Trump opposed transparency reverses his public position on the warrant, while the affidavit dispute involved the government’s position and a media coalition’s motion. The judge later ordered a redacted affidavit released, while sensitive names and investigative details remained withheld. The episode unfolded through separate filings over the warrant, the property receipt and the evidentiary affidavit.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "DOJ Moves to Unseal Mar-a-Lago Search Warrant—Trump Opposes Transparency", "reason": "Corrects the central premise: Trump said he would not oppose unsealing the warrant, while DOJ separately opposed unsealing the affidavit."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.latimes.com/politics/story/2022-08-11/trump-calls-for-immediate-release-of-mar-a-lago-warrant", "https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2022/08/15/trump-mar-a-lago-affidavit/", "https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2022/08/25/trump-affidavit-mar-a-lago/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-12-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["Used contemporaneous coverage distinguishing the warrant and property receipt from the later affidavit dispute; the NBC headline alone could blur the two."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Corrects the central premise: Trump said he would not oppose unsealing the warrant, while DOJ separately opposed unsealing the affidavit.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 55, "description_long": 186}}
{"record_id": "entry-6047", "entry_number": 6047, "assignment_position": 1119, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Trump Calls Democrats’ COVID-19 Response Criticism a “New Hoax”", "description_medium": "At a February 28, 2020 rally in South Carolina, Trump called Democrats’ criticism of his coronavirus response their “new hoax.” His remarks came as COVID-19 cases were being reported in the United States. The next day he said he meant political criticism of his response, not that the virus itself was a hoax; the distinction is essential to describing the event.", "description_long": "Trump used the phrase “new hoax” at a campaign rally in North Charleston, South Carolina, on February 28, 2020, while attacking Democrats for criticizing his administration’s handling of the emerging coronavirus outbreak. In the same speech he said Democrats were politicizing the outbreak and compared their criticism with earlier political controversies. The wording prompted widespread debate over whether he had dismissed COVID-19 itself. The following day, Trump clarified that he meant Democrats’ criticism of his response, not the virus. That clarification does not erase the broader record of his public minimization of the threat, but it changes what this specific phrase referred to. The rally took place amid the first confirmed U.S. cases and before the World Health Organization declared a pandemic in March. The supplied title and synopsis conflate criticism of the administration with the disease, and make a causal claim that the rally caused delays or deaths. The event-specific sources support the quotation and context, not that causal conclusion; a careful account keeps the statement, its target and the subsequent clarification distinct.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "Trump Dismisses COVID-19 as Democratic Hoax, Delays Response", "reason": "Corrects the common misquotation by identifying what Trump called a hoax and includes his next-day clarification; removes unsupported causal claims about the response timeline."}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/ebc90501a013f52d792e72069bd4ad7f", "https://www.factcheck.org/2020/03/trump-and-the-new-hoax/", "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-keep-america-great-rally-charleston-south-carolina"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/chatgpt-web/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-02-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["Used AP’s rally report, the full speech transcript and FactCheck.org’s account of Trump’s February 29 clarification."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Corrects the common misquotation by identifying what Trump called a hoax and includes his next-day clarification; removes unsupported causal claims about the response timeline.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 61, "description_long": 174}}
{"record_id": "entry-6072", "entry_number": 6072, "assignment_position": 1121, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Giuliani Holds Election-Fraud Press Conference at Four Seasons Total Landscaping", "description_medium": "On November 7, 2020, Rudy Giuliani and Trump campaign officials held a press conference outside Four Seasons Total Landscaping in Philadelphia, not at a Four Seasons hotel. Giuliani repeated claims of election fraud as networks projected Joe Biden the winner. The setting attracted attention, but the venue itself does not establish whether the campaign’s legal claims had merit.", "description_long": "The Trump campaign announced a Philadelphia press conference on November 7, 2020, as major television networks projected Joe Biden to win the presidential election. The event took place in the parking lot of Four Seasons Total Landscaping, a small business in Northeast Philadelphia, rather than at the Four Seasons hotel many people initially assumed the campaign meant. Rudy Giuliani, then a lawyer for Trump, spoke about legal challenges to the Pennsylvania vote and asserted that the campaign had evidence of widespread fraud. The location quickly drew national attention because of the name confusion and its commercial surroundings. The event’s visual setting became a running joke, but the substantive issue was the campaign’s attempt to contest the count after losing the election. The claims Giuliani made were allegations, not proof that fraud changed the outcome. Giuliani said the campaign would continue legal challenges in Pennsylvania and that Trump would not concede. The hearing did not itself adjudicate those claims, and the venue’s name confusion remained distinct from the election dispute.", "corrected_wording": null, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-joe-biden-donald-trump-georgia-elections-a7b4aa4d8ce3bf52301ddbe620c6bff6", "https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/style/four-seasons-total-landscaping-guiliani-trump-election/2020/11/08/3cf80056-2134-11eb-b532-05c751cd5dc2_story.html", "https://www.inquirer.com/news/four-seasons-landscaping-philly-trump-giuliani-hotel-biden-20201108.html"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/chatgpt-web/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-04-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["Used AP’s event report and Philadelphia/Washington Post coverage for the venue and context; removed the synopsis’s unsupported editorial characterization."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Keeps the memorable venue fact while separating Giuliani’s allegations from established election results and avoiding subjective claims about competence.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 58, "description_long": 169}}
{"record_id": "entry-6100", "entry_number": 6100, "assignment_position": 1123, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Trump Envoy Sets a 100-Day Goal for a Ukraine Peace Deal", "description_medium": "In January 2025, Trump’s incoming Ukraine envoy Keith Kellogg said he hoped to help end the war within 100 days of the administration taking office. Trump had previously promised a faster resolution, including a “Day One” pledge. This was an envoy’s target, not a public ultimatum giving Ukraine 100 days to negotiate or lose U.S. support.", "description_long": "During the transition in January 2025, Keith Kellogg, Trump’s incoming special envoy for Ukraine and Russia, said he hoped a solution to the war could be reached within 100 days of Trump taking office. Reuters reported that the timeline was a retreat from Trump’s campaign promise to end the conflict on his first day back in the White House. Kellogg’s statement set an objective for U.S. diplomacy; it did not impose a deadline on Ukraine or announce that U.S. military and financial assistance would end after 100 days. Trump was inaugurated on January 20, and the war continued as the administration pursued contacts with Kyiv and Moscow. The target differed from Trump’s earlier campaign promise to end the war on his first day in office: Kellogg described a diplomatic objective measured in months. The administration would need to engage both Kyiv and Moscow, whose goals diverged on territory and security guarantees. The 100-day estimate set expectations for negotiations, but it did not describe an agreement already reached or a deadline Ukraine had accepted.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "Trump Issues 100-Day Ukraine Ultimatum: Negotiate or Lose US Support", "reason": "Separates the January envoy timeline from the November 2025 ultimatum, correcting both actor and event while retaining the documented 100-day goal."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/trump-advisers-concede-ukraine-peace-deal-is-months-away-2025-01-15/", "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-01-11/donald-trump-s-ukraine-peace-envoy-vows-to-end-war-in-100-days/104806942"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/lightsprint/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-07-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["The linked November 2025 CNN story is a different negotiation episode; used January reporting on Kellogg’s 100-day goal and distinguished it from Trump’s later proposal deadline."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Separates the January envoy timeline from the November 2025 ultimatum, correcting both actor and event while retaining the documented 100-day goal.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 56, "description_long": 173}}
{"record_id": "entry-6104", "entry_number": 6104, "assignment_position": 1125, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Trump’s 28-Point Ukraine Plan Would Recognize Russian Control of Territory", "description_medium": "In November 2025, the Associated Press reported on a 28-point Trump administration draft peace proposal. It called for U.S. recognition of Russian control over Crimea, Donetsk and Luhansk, and a freeze along the line of contact in Kherson and Zaporizhzhia. The proposal’s terms were de facto recognition and a frozen line, not a simple formal cession of 20 percent of Ukraine.", "description_long": "The Associated Press published details of a 28-point draft proposal to end Russia’s war against Ukraine on November 21, 2025. The draft, which AP said Washington and Moscow had worked on, called for U.S. recognition of Russian control over Crimea, Donetsk and Luhansk, while freezing control lines in Kherson and Zaporizhzhia. Other reported provisions included limits on the size of Ukraine’s military, a commitment against NATO membership and a rapid timetable for elections. Ukrainian officials and European governments objected to provisions they viewed as favoring Russia and undermining Ukraine’s security. These were proposed terms under discussion, not a signed ceasefire or an agreement accepted by Kyiv. The draft did not describe every occupied area in the same legal way: some would be recognized as de facto Russian, while others would be frozen along the existing line of contact. The draft also called for an immediate ceasefire after agreement and proposed oversight by a Peace Council chaired by Trump. Those terms remained a proposal rather than an agreement accepted by Ukraine, Russia or European governments.", "corrected_wording": {"prior_title": "Trump Proposes Ukraine Cede 20% of Its Territory to Russia for Ceasefire", "reason": "Corrects the title’s oversimplification by distinguishing proposed de facto recognition from frozen front lines and a completed territorial transfer."}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/92b2c89b62f4326ff67abc5613c9b59e", "https://www.axios.com/2025/11/20/trump-ukraine-peace-plan-28-points-russia"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-09-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["Used AP’s reporting and text of the draft; the source does not support describing every territorial provision as a formal cession."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Corrects the title’s oversimplification by distinguishing proposed de facto recognition from frozen front lines and a completed territorial transfer.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 61, "description_long": 174}}
